he rings are originally forged and  owned solely by the elves who create  them under the guidance of a disguised  sauron  later when war breaks out between the  elves and sauron the dark lord gathers  all but three of them up and  redistributes them to middle earth's  more easily conquered races  while it's almost certain that the rings  are the great powers mentioned here it  should also be pointed out that the  amazon synopsis could be talking about  something else  specifically middle earth's great  alliances the third age is largely  defined by the free peoples of middle  earth fighting sauron in isolated  pockets but the second age actually  features several major unions  particularly between the races of elves  men and dwarves  the age even ends with the famous last  alliance which takes the battle to  mordor and defeats sauron during the  siege of our door  one phrase from the synopsis that jumps  out is the suggestion that during the  second age  kingdoms rose to glory while there are  many strange new places that the amazon  show will doubtless spend time exploring  several of these rising kingdoms will be  quite familiar or familiar in name at  least  during this period galadriel visits her  future kingdom in lorien for the first  time  while legolas grandfather orifir  establishes a refugee elven kingdom in  the heart of the greenwood the dwarving  kingdom of kazadoom is already ancient  when the second age begins and it plays  a vital role in the politics of western  middle earth gondor is founded toward  the end of the second h2  and elrond establishes rivendell around  halfway through in direct defiance of an  all-powerful  sauron and as for the dark lord himself  in need of a home after his return to  middle earth he carves out a corner of  the map for himself  in the dark and terrible land known as  mordor  amazon synopsis mentions that the second  age is a time in which unlikely heroes  were tested this is a vague yet  tantalizing glimpse into the show's cast  of characters  which for now remains largely shrouded  in mystery sure a few hints have come  out here and there for instance ian  mckellen has said he's up for reprising  his role as gandalf even though the  character doesn't arrive in middle earth  until the third age  on the flip side elrond actor hugo  weaving has made it abundantly clear he  has no plans to come back to tolkien's  world and although many of the show's  cast members have already been announced  for the most part we don't yet know for  sure who's playing who  or if the characters they're playing  have any relation to heroes and villains  we've already seen on screen before  the most solid actor to character  connection that has leaked so far is  that welsh actress morphed clark will be  playing a younger galadriel other  casting leagues have only added to the  confusion by throwing around brand new  character names with no obvious relation  to established tolkien texts  according to an october 2019 report by  deadline english actor joseph maul will  be playing a character named oren who is  intriguingly described as being the lead  villain of the series  other new characters reportedly include  the heroic bellador and a female lead  named tyra played by marquella cavanaugh  it was initially supposed by fans that  these were fake names disguising  well-known characters  however the synopsis states that the  series follows an ensemble cast of  characters both familiar and  new so these could well be the actual  names of brand new characters that have  been made up for the show  unfortunately the new synopsis doesn't  offer up any kind of definitive answer  regarding which heroes will be taken on  the dark lord  it does however suggest that the show  will be bringing in some genuinely fresh  faces with no obvious roots in lord of  the rings lore whether the fans like it  or not  in the lord of the rings gandalf implies  that sauron's potential return wouldn't  be the first time middle earth has been  dominated by the dark lord  sauron needs only this ring to cover all  the lands in a second darkness  so if that's the second darkness that  he's talking about when exactly was the  first  why in the second age of course the  amazon synopsis mentions that at this  time  hope hung by the finest of threads and  the greatest villain that ever flowed  from tolkien's pen  threatened to cover all the world in  darkness these words suggest that the  amazon series will portray sauron's  initial rise to power  including his greatest victories and  near total conquest of all of  middle-earth of course this doesn't just  give amazon the opportunity to put the  show's heroes through the ringer it also  allows the studio to divest him of the  one-dimensional persona that defines him  throughout the lore of the rings  instead the show is likely to introduce  fans to a much more interesting version  of tolkien's iconic villain  sauron's key role in the synopsis  suggests what bands have believed for a  long time that the show will depict the  dark lord in his fairer form while the  sauron of the lord of the rings is a  hideous monstrous figure  or a giant dye if you're watching the  movies he was once able to make himself  appear  well pretty darn sexy more specifically  sauron spends much of the second age  masquerading as anatar the lord of gifts  this charismatic disguise allows the  dark lord to mingle with the elves and  for a while  gain their trust sauron remains in his  fair form for much of this time and only  loses the ability to look like this when  numenor is destroyed towards the end of  the second age  obviously sauron's prettier persona  doesn't make him trustworthy by any  means as yells soon discover for  themselves  nonetheless the chance to betray the  dark lord in this way will likely add  another fascinating layer to a villain  not often associated with nuance or  depth  there's no question that sauron is the  biggest threat to middle earth during  the second and third ages but that  doesn't mean he's the only monster  hiding in the shadows  notably the final sentence of the leaked  synopsis begins with the line from the  darkest depths of the misty mountains  this line has some fascinating  implications for the direction the show  might take  it suggests that viewers will be taken  deeper than the thriving halls of kaza  doom to explore the terrible unexplored  spaces beneath the dwarven kingdom  so what's actually down there well for  one thing there's the balrog known as  durian's bane  you know what they awoke in the darkness  of hazard  shadow and flame this fiery demon isn't  just a terror to all and sundry it's  also fiercely independent having found  itself without a master after the  destruction of morgoth  as such although durin's bane is  supposed to be asleep for much of the  second age it could still turn out to be  an important part of the amazon series  but the malrog isn't the only thing  living beneath the misty mountains  when gandalf discusses his battle under  moria and the two towers  he mentions that far far below the  deepest delvings of the dwarves the  world is gnawed by nameless things  even sauron knows them not they are  older than he considering sauron and the  balrogs fought together in the first age  it's clear that gandalf is referring to  something altogether more horrifying  than durian's bane  and while toki never elaborated on what  creatures lurk in those darkest depths  it seems that the show may just tackle  that question for itself  one of the more specific lines in the  synopsis is the brief mention that the  story will span to the majestic force of  the elf capital of lindon this naturally  leads to the question  what and where is lindon the short  answer is that lindon  is an important elven nation that exists  throughout the second age  more important than that though lindon  is a very complex and important place  that plays a vital role in many of  middle earth's stories  it sits in the western reaches of the  continent right next to the area that  eventually becomes the shire  it's also the place that grey havens are  located which is where frodo and bilbo  sail from into the west at the end of  return of the king  in the second age lindon is one of the  most powerful kingdoms in middle earth  as sauron's darkness spreads across the  world it becomes a beacon of hope  to the free peoples who stand against  the dark lord ruled by the elven king  gilgalad  lindon also plays a key role in forging  crucial alliances with men and other  elves and ultimately provides much of  the muscle needed to topple sauron at  the end of the age  the last sentence of the synopsis states  that the show will also take viewers to  the breathtaking island kingdom of  numenor  the involvement of this island nation of  men was a foregone conclusion from the  first moment the second age was  confirmed  nevertheless knowing that numenor will  get its fair share of the spotlight  as exciting news for fans of tolkien's  legendarium the godlike beings known as  the valar give the island to its  inhabitants early in the second age and  the men of numenor continued to flourish  there for centuries  the men of numenor eventually get  tangled up with the events on the  mainland  and for a while become the focus of  sauron's obsession unfortunately the  dark lord's machinations ultimately lead  to the total destruction of the island  and the near extinction of its people  one of the final lines in the synopsis  reveals that the show's characters will  go to the furthest reaches of the map  this is a particularly interesting  statement as it can basically mean one  of two things  this could be a veiled reference to the  blessed realm of oman the land that lies  hidden in the west  this is where the valar and the elves  live in blissful harmony and it's also  where middle  earth's elves sail after their time on  the continent comes to an end  otherwise known 